+## Phase 2: Foundational (Blocking Prerequisites)
+
+**Purpose**: The store lifecycle, the error channel and the CLI entry point — every user story
+needs all three
+
+**⚠️ CRITICAL**: No user story work can begin until this phase is complete
+
+- [ ] T004 Define `BookmarkError(Exception)` in `src/bookmarks/store.py` carrying `message` and `status` fields, per research R-008: one exception type, raised at the point of detection, with the exit status as data rather than three near-empty subclasses
+- [ ] T005 Add the schema to `src/bookmarks/store.py` as the two `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` statements quoted verbatim from data-model.md: `bookmarks (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, url TEXT NOT NULL, title TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '')` and `tags (bookmark_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES bookmarks(id) ON DELETE CASCADE, tag TEXT NOT NULL, PRIMARY KEY (bookmark_id, tag))`. `AUTOINCREMENT` is load-bearing for FR-006 (never-reused identifiers) and the composite primary key enforces FR-010's at-most-once rule in storage — neither may be dropped. Add no index on `tags(tag)` and no `UNIQUE` on `bookmarks.url` (data-model.md, research R-006)
+- [ ] T006 Implement `open_store(path)` in `src/bookmarks/store.py` as the exact three-step sequence of research R-002 (FR-002, FR-003, FR-003a): percent-encode the path before building the SQLite URI so a `?` or `#` in it is not read as URI syntax; if the file is absent open `file:<path>?mode=rwc` and run the schema; if it exists open `file:<path>?mode=rw` (never `rwc`) and immediately run `SELECT name FROM sqlite_master WHERE type='table'` — `sqlite3.DatabaseError` → `BookmarkError(status=1)` "not a bookmark database"; a non-empty table set containing neither `bookmarks` nor `tags` → `BookmarkError(status=1)` "another program's database"; an empty table set → initialise the schema and continue; our tables → use as-is. Map `sqlite3.OperationalError` from connect or first query (missing parent directory, permission denied) to `BookmarkError(status=1)`. Run `PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON` on every connection, since SQLite leaves foreign keys off by default and the `ON DELETE CASCADE` would otherwise do nothing. Every diagnostic names the path **and** the reason (Principle II). Create no file and no directory on any failing branch, and never overwrite, truncate, re-initialise or repair an existing file
+- [ ] T007 [P] Implement `build_parser()` in `src/bookmarks/cli.py`: `ArgumentParser(prog="python -m bookmarks")` — `prog` must be set explicitly because argparse's default under `-m` is the literal `__main__.py`, which would misname the tool in every diagnostic (research R-003) — the global option `--db PATH` defaulting to `bookmarks.db` in the current working directory and declared **before** the command (FR-002, CLI contract), and `add_subparsers(dest="command", required=True)`. Register no subcommands yet; each user story adds its own. Do **not** override `ArgumentParser.error`: its default already writes a specific diagnostic to stderr and exits 2, which is exactly what FR-024 demands (research R-003)
+- [ ] T008 Implement `main(argv)` in `src/bookmarks/cli.py`: reconfigure the streams once at the top with `sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8", newline="")` and `sys.stderr.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8")` (FR-023a, FR-019a, research R-007); parse argv; dispatch to the command handler; and wrap the dispatch in exactly **one** `except BookmarkError` handler that writes `f"bookmarks: {message}"` to stderr and returns `status`. Results are written to stdout only after the work succeeds, so a failing command emits no partial output (FR-023). Do not catch `sqlite3.Error` broadly — unexpected database faults must propagate and crash rather than be folded into one of the four documented statuses (research R-008)
+- [ ] T009 Create `src/bookmarks/__main__.py` containing only `sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))` plus its import, so `python -m bookmarks` is the real entry point with `src/` on `PYTHONPATH` (FR-001, FR-026)
+- [ ] T010 [P] Create `tests/conftest.py` with three fixtures used by every test file: a scratch-store fixture giving a fresh `tmp_path`-based `--db` path per test; a `run_cli` helper invoking the real CLI as a subprocess — `subprocess.run([sys.executable, "-m", "bookmarks", ...], env={**os.environ, "PYTHONPATH": "src"}, capture_output=True)` — returning status, stdout and stderr, because an in-process call cannot observe a real process exit status (research R-009); and a bulk seeder that inserts N bookmarks through `store.py` directly rather than through N subprocess calls
+- [ ] T011 Write the store-lifecycle tests in `tests/test_store.py`, exercising `store.open_store` directly (no subcommand needed yet) and naming the requirement each test covers: default path is `bookmarks.db` in the working directory and `--db` overrides it (FR-002); an absent path with an existing parent is created with its schema (FR-003); an existing store is opened and appended to, never reset (FR-003); a path whose parent directory is missing raises status 1 and creates **no** directory and **no** file (FR-003a); a plain text file raises status 1 and is byte-for-byte unchanged afterwards, asserted by comparing SHA-256 before and after (FR-003a, SC-009); a valid SQLite database holding another program's tables raises status 1 and is left untouched (FR-003a, SC-009); and a permission-denied case (`chmod 000`) that **skips itself** under `os.geteuid() == 0` with an explicit skip reason, because root bypasses the mode bits and the test would otherwise pass without proving anything (research R-002 test-environment caveat)
+
+**Checkpoint**: `PYTHONPATH=src python -m bookmarks --help` exits 0, bare `python -m bookmarks`
+exits 2, and `tests/test_store.py` passes. User story work can now begin.
