+## Phase 3: User Story 1 - Save a bookmark and get it back later (Priority: P1) 🎯 MVP
+
+**Goal**: `add` stores a bookmark and prints its identifier; `list` returns every bookmark in a
+later process with title and tags intact. Capture and review — a usable tool on its own.
+
+**Independent Test**: Run `add` twice against a fresh empty store, then run `list` in a separate
+invocation: the first prints `1`, the second `2`, and `list` prints two four-field TSV lines in
+ascending identifier order. Also add a bookmark whose URL fails the scheme check and confirm exit
+2 with nothing stored. Needs neither search, delete nor export to exist.
+
+### Tests for User Story 1
+
+> **Write these tests FIRST and confirm they FAIL before implementing T014–T020.**
+
+- [ ] T012 [P] [US1] Write `tests/test_add.py` covering FR-005 … FR-010 through `run_cli`, each test naming its requirement: `add URL` prints the identifier **and nothing else** on stdout (FR-005); identifiers start at 1 in a fresh store and increase (FR-006); a URL not beginning with the literal `http://` or `https://` writes a diagnostic to stderr naming the rejected URL **and** the accepted schemes, stores nothing, writes nothing to stdout, and exits 2 — parametrised over at least five malformed inputs: `ftp://example.com`, bare `example.com`, the empty string, `"see https://x"` (scheme not at the start), and `HTTP://example.com` (FR-007, SC-004); `--title TITLE` sets the title and omitting it yields the empty string, never `NULL` and never a placeholder (FR-008); `--tags` is comma-separated and omitting it yields no tags (FR-009); and the full normalisation table from data-model.md asserted case by case — `python,cli` → `cli,python`, `python,,docs` → `docs,python`, `""` → none, `"  python  ,  docs "` → `docs,python`, `Python,python,PYTHON` → `python`, `Python,Docs` → `docs,python` (FR-010)
+- [ ] T013 [P] [US1] Write the unfiltered-listing tests in `tests/test_list_search.py` covering FR-011, FR-014, FR-015 and FR-004: `list` prints every bookmark in ascending identifier order (FR-011); each line is **exactly four tab-separated fields** — identifier, URL, title, tags joined by `,` in alphabetical order — with an empty title and an empty tags field printed as empty text so a bookmark with no title and no tags ends in two consecutive tabs and the line still carries three tabs (FR-014); an empty store prints nothing on stdout and exits 0 (FR-015); and bookmarks added by earlier, separate processes are returned unchanged by a later one (FR-004). Include a title containing a tab and a title containing a newline, asserting they are stored and returned **unchanged** — the TSV view is deliberately unescaped and therefore ambiguous for such values (CLI contract, spec Assumptions)
+
+### Implementation for User Story 1
+
+- [ ] T014 [US1] Implement `normalise_tags(raw)` in `src/bookmarks/store.py` performing the five steps in exactly the order data-model.md specifies: split on `,`, strip surrounding whitespace from each entry, drop entries that are empty after stripping, lower-case each remaining entry, then collapse duplicates. No stemming, no synonyms, no Unicode form folding beyond `str.lower()`. This one function must later normalise the `--tag` filter value too, which is what guarantees the two sides can never disagree (FR-010, FR-012)
+- [ ] T015 [US1] Implement the URL scheme check in `src/bookmarks/store.py` as a literal prefix test for `http://` or `https://` — no parsing, no host validation, no reachability check (spec Assumptions) — raising `BookmarkError(status=2)` whose message names the rejected URL and the accepted schemes. The check must run **before** any insert so a rejected URL leaves the store exactly as it was (FR-007)
+- [ ] T016 [US1] Implement `add_bookmark(connection, url, title, tags)` in `src/bookmarks/store.py`: run the scheme check, insert into `bookmarks` with `title` defaulting to `''`, insert one row per normalised tag into `tags`, commit, and return the new identifier from `AUTOINCREMENT` (FR-005, FR-006, FR-008, FR-009, FR-010). Do not de-duplicate on `url` — the same address may be saved more than once, each time receiving its own identifier (spec Edge Cases)
+- [ ] T017 [US1] Implement `list_bookmarks(connection)` in `src/bookmarks/store.py` returning every bookmark in ascending `id` order, each with its tags sorted by Python's default string ordering (ordinary alphabetical order, since stored tags are always lower case) (FR-011, FR-014)
+- [ ] T018 [US1] Implement the TSV renderer in `src/bookmarks/cli.py`: one line per bookmark, exactly four tab-separated fields in the order identifier, URL, title, comma-joined alphabetical tags, emitted raw with **no escaping** (FR-014, CLI contract). This renderer is shared with `search` in US2 — write it once
+- [ ] T019 [US1] Register the `add` subparser and its handler in `src/bookmarks/cli.py`: positional `url`, optional `--title` defaulting to `""`, optional `--tags`. The handler opens the store, calls `add_bookmark`, and prints the identifier followed by a newline to stdout and nothing else (FR-005, FR-008, FR-009)
+- [ ] T020 [US1] Register the `list` subparser and its handler in `src/bookmarks/cli.py` with no options yet (US2 adds `--tag`): open the store, call `list_bookmarks`, render through T018's renderer to stdout, and exit 0 even when there is nothing to print (FR-011, FR-014, FR-015)
+- [ ] T021 [US1] Extend `tests/test_store.py` with the subprocess-level store-failure assertions now that `add` and `list` exist: `--db` pointing into a missing parent directory, at a plain text file, and at another program's SQLite database each exits **1** with the diagnostic on stderr and **nothing on stdout**, for both a read command (`list`) and a write command (`add`), with the target file's SHA-256 unchanged afterwards (FR-003a, SC-009). Include the documented consequence that a read-only store file fails with status 1 even for `list`, because all commands open the store read-write (research R-002, CLI contract)
