+## Phase 5: User Story 3 - Remove a bookmark I no longer want (Priority: P3)
+
+**Goal**: Delete by identifier so the bookmark appears in no later listing, search or export, with
+a missing identifier reported as a mistake rather than silently accepted — and the identifier
+stays retired.
+
+**Independent Test**: Add bookmarks, delete one by identifier, list the remainder; separately
+delete an identifier that was never issued (exit 3), delete an already-deleted one (exit 3), and
+delete a non-numeric one (exit 2).
+
+### Tests for User Story 3
+
+> **Write these tests FIRST and confirm they FAIL before implementing T028–T029.**
+
+- [ ] T027 [P] [US3] Write `tests/test_delete.py` covering FR-016, FR-017 and FR-006: `delete ID` exits 0 and the bookmark, together with its tags, appears in no later `list`, `search` or `export` (FR-016, verifying the `ON DELETE CASCADE` actually fired — the tag rows must be gone, which is only true if `PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON` is set); deleting an identifier no bookmark holds writes a diagnostic **naming that identifier** to stderr, leaves the store unchanged, and exits **3** (FR-017); deleting an already-deleted identifier also exits 3 (US3 scenario 3); after deleting the highest identifier, the next `add` receives an identifier **greater than every identifier previously issued** — delete bookmark 2 of 2 and the next add must print `3`, not `2`, which is the single most likely thing to regress (FR-006, US3 scenario 4); and `delete abc` exits **2**, never 3, because a non-numeric identifier is a usage mistake while 3 means well-formed but absent (FR-024, data-model.md)
+- [ ] T028 [US3] Implement `delete_bookmark(connection, id)` in `src/bookmarks/store.py`: issue the `DELETE`, and when the affected row count is 0 raise `BookmarkError(status=3)` with a message naming the identifier; otherwise commit, letting `ON DELETE CASCADE` remove the tag rows in one declaration rather than a second `DELETE` that could drift (FR-016, FR-017, research R-006)
+- [ ] T029 [US3] Register the `delete` subparser and its handler in `src/bookmarks/cli.py`: positional `id` declared with `type=int` so argparse itself produces the precise diagnostic and status 2 for a non-numeric value, keeping status 3 strictly reserved for the well-formed-but-absent case (research R-003). The handler prints nothing to stdout on success and exits 0 (CLI contract)
+
+**Checkpoint**: User Stories 1–3 all work independently — quickstart scenario 4 passes.
